        <article class="section-card" id="cloud-onboard">
          <span class="section-badge">TECHNICAL RECAP · GOOGLE CLOUD ONBOARD: GOOGLE SKILLS EDITION (WESTIN JOSUN PARNAS)</span>
          <h2>Scaling Multi-Agent Services on Vertex AI · Google Cloud OnBoard Notes</h2>
          <p>
            Engineering notes from <strong>Google Cloud OnBoard: Google Skills Edition</strong> (Westin Josun Parnas, Seoul), documenting the architectural transition from single-session hackathon prototypes (<strong>GeminiSpace</strong>) to stateful multi-agent production runtimes (<strong>Gouncil</strong> and <strong>m9g-agent-corps</strong>).
          </p>

          <h3>1. Multimodal Model Routing in Vertex AI Model Garden</h3>
          <p>
            Technical sessions led by Google Cloud and Google DeepMind engineers covered unified serving across 200+ managed endpoints in <strong>Vertex AI Model Garden</strong>, pairing <strong>Gemini 3</strong> and open-weight <strong>Gemma 3</strong> models with <strong>GenMedia</strong> pipelines (<strong>Veo</strong> video generation and <strong>Lyria</strong> audio synthesis) under centralized quota and latency telemetry.
          </p>

          <h3>2. Track 1: Agent Development Kit (ADK), MCP vs. A2A &amp; Cloud Run</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Protocol Separation (MCP vs. A2A):</strong> The <strong>Model Context Protocol (MCP)</strong> binds an individual agent to deterministic tools, databases, and REST APIs, whereas the <strong>Agent2Agent (A2A)</strong> protocol coordinates asynchronous task handoffs and capability discovery across separate autonomous agents. Separating tool execution (MCP) from peer delegation (A2A) directly shaped the routing layer in <strong>Gouncil</strong>.<br />
            <strong>Stateless Compute on Cloud Run:</strong> Deploying LangGraph and <strong>ADK</strong> workers as autoscaling containers on <strong>Cloud Run</strong> backed by <strong>AlloyDB</strong> or <strong>Cloud SQL</strong> vector indexes and <strong>Cloud Build</strong> CI/CD pipelines isolates bursty agent inference from persistent state.
          </p>

          <h3>3. Four-Layer Vertex AI Memory Architecture</h3>
          <p>Generative AI Field Solution Architect Sang-woon So detailed a four-tier memory partition that bounds prompt token growth across long-horizon multi-turn sessions:</p>
          <table class="spec-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Memory Layer</th><th>Cognitive Role &amp; Vertex AI Implementation</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td><strong>1. Session Memory (Short-Term)</strong></td><td>Stores active turn state, intermediate reasoning traces, and tool return payloads within a live session.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>2. Personalized Memory Bank (Long-Term)</strong></td><td>Persists structured user preferences, recurring constraints, and cross-session summaries across interactions.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>3. External Information Grounding</strong></td><td>Queries live web facts and geospatial state through Google Search and Google Maps grounding.</td></tr>
              <tr><td><strong>4. Internal Information Retrieval</strong></td><td>Retrieves private domain documents via Vertex AI RAG Engine and ScaNN approximate nearest-neighbor vector search.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </article>
